  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "xu-zhimo-poems-s0037-p00037",
      "source_en": "The autumn rain falls in a stream of clear, cold water in the autumn pond. On a withered autumn willow, on a timid, piteous autumn branch, on a leaf just not yet turned yellow, one listens to him whispering tenderly, murmur to murmur, spouting intimacies of autumnal love—thoughts, affairs, lines of passion, scenes of emotion. At last, he softly brushes it off into the autumn glow of the water’s waves, circling half a swirl, and follows away with the autumn current. These whisperings of the autumn rain, this three-autumn tale of longing and love—its thoughts, its love-words, its little drama—too falls into the autumn mist of rippling water, turns half a swirl, and drifts away with the autumn flow. July 21",
